/-!
# Removing the central kernel of the full transvection product

The actual covering group P/O₂(P) is generated by involutions and has
trivial two-core. Its central kernel therefore has odd order. Over each
marked S₃ or S₅ factor, the odd central extension splits by the Coxeter
relations. The complement is characteristic because the quotient is
generated by two-elements. Over L₃(2), the derived subgroup of the preimage
is a perfect central cover; cyclic odd Sylow transfer and the trivial
two-core make its projection an isomorphism.

These characteristic complements are normal in the original covering
group. Their images commute, and injectivity on each complement makes
the complements themselves commute. The product gives a section of the
original covering map. Its quotient has odd order, so generation by
involutions forces the section to be surjective and the kernel to vanish.
The product coordinates are exactly the literal full preimages of the
marked factors.

The escape application retains the same W/Z_S action, ambient subnormal
embedding and residual-preimage identities as NaturalGeometryAssembly.
Its action kernel is exactly O₂(P), with no new geometric premise.
This proves the central-kernel step of Aschbacher–Smith 6.1.19, printed
p.675. Cubic normalization of the full factors and the amalgam contradiction,
initial 6.1.4–5 and the remaining escape geometry stay with
qt-d-1222-rankone-recognition-natural; unconditional 6.1.27 is not asserted.
The reused splitting and transfer proofs document their sources in
OddCoverLifting/SymmetricSplitting and PSL3Two/PerfectCentralCover.
-/

